{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_1082813673#6_1510639763","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is focused on e-waste being shipped to developing countries and directly supports a key recycling challenge: exported/imported e-waste is processed in regions where lead, mercury, and other heavy metals contaminate soil and water, creating serious health and environmental risks.","learned":"A key challenge is preventing e-waste exports to developing countries where processing contaminates soil and water with heavy metals such as lead and mercury."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1632","reason":"This query found direct evidence on why e-waste recycling/disposal is difficult and hazardous, so continue for more documents that explicitly discuss difficulty, hazardous materials, and mixed material recovery."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1632"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1957491344#3_3169262515","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1632","rank":2,"snippet":"How to recycle VHS tapes: Why are VHS tapes hard to recycle? Can I sell or donate old VHS tapes? \" , \"segment\": \"In general, most cities do not accept VHS tapes in curbside recycling, but some do. At the same time, some municipalities might make exceptions or else have special <cover>e-waste recycling days where residents can drop off old VHS tapes or even VCRs. Article continues below advertisement Source: Getty Images Why are VHS tapes hard to recycle? According to Earth911, VHS tapes are difficult to recycle not because of the outside casing, but the magnetic media inside. The casing on VHS tapes is mostly made of No. 5 plastic or polypropylene, which can be recycled. The inside, on the other hand, is mylar tape, made of No. 1 plastic, which is coated in an assortment of metals </cover>that are not so easy to recycle. It’s not that this mylar isn’t inherently reusable, it’s just that no one really wants it.\" , \"start_char\": 1803.000000 , \"title\": \"How to Recycle VHS Tapes\" , \"url\": \"https://"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"14/50"}}
