{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_31_1536260291#4_3126599563","relevance":1,"reason":"The document is a commercial electronics/e-waste recycling and disposal service page. It is related to e-waste recycling and mentions special e-waste events and checking whether trash collection accepts electronics, but it does not identify or explain key recycling challenges such as low recycling rates, awareness, or collection barriers.","learned":"Service pages may mention special collection routes for e-waste, but they are not usually strong evidence for systemic recycling challenges."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"e-waste\" (+ \"low recycling rate\" \"recycling rate\" \"only recycled\") (+ collection awareness challenge))","reason":"Refine the low-rate/collection-barrier search by forcing explicit recycling-rate language with e-waste and collection/awareness/challenge terms, aiming for documents that directly discuss poor collection and participation as key challenges."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"e-waste\" (+ \"low recycling rate\" \"recycling rate\" \"only recycled\") (+ collection awareness challenge))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_417129682#12_1002533590","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1643","rank":1,"snippet":"minimum recycling targets based on their sales. The problem with a patchwork of laws is that no one state has enough market share to compel manufacturers to design greener or more durable products. By contrast, the European Union’s Restriction of Hazardous Substances Directive represents the entire EU market and thus has the clout to set higher standards for all electronic products sold in the EU. Its laws requiring manufacturers to help pay for recycling have resulted in an e-waste <cover>recycling rate of 35 percent, higher than that of the U.S. A federal law in the U.S. could help develop a more robust e-waste recycling infrastructure through setting targets and establishing financing schemes for collection </cover>systems and recycling plants. It could also offer a tax break or rebate to companies that process their used devices, and help prevent the export of e-waste to developing countries. However, a federal law is not likely to happen under this administration. A problem requiring multiple solutions With the flood of e-waste growing around the world, recycling alone will not be enough.\" , \"start_char\": 7230.000000 , \"title\": \""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"28/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_417129682#12_1002533590","relevance":3,"reason":"The document directly discusses e-waste recycling rates and systemic challenges, including fragmented laws, insufficient recycling infrastructure, financing needs for collection systems and recycling plants, low U.S. performance compared with the EU, and preventing export of e-waste to developing countries.","learned":"Key challenges include low recycling rates, patchwork regulation, lack of robust collection/recycling infrastructure and financing, and continued export of e-waste to developing countries."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1643","reason":"This query produced strong direct evidence on low recycling rates, collection systems, regulation, infrastructure, and exports, so continue to find more grade-3 evidence in this challenge area."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1643"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_417129682#13_1002535867","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1643","rank":2,"snippet":"recycling Better recycling The goal of a circular economy What you can do about e-waste Resources 60 Minutes: The Wasteland (e-waste in China) Tags: \" , \"segment\": \"By contrast, the European Union’s Restriction of Hazardous Substances Directive represents the entire EU market and thus has the clout to set higher standards for all electronic products sold in the EU. Its laws requiring manufacturers to help pay for recycling have resulted in an e-waste <cover>recycling rate of 35 percent, higher than that of the U.S. A federal law in the U.S. could help develop a more robust e-waste recycling infrastructure through setting targets and establishing financing schemes for collection </cover>systems and recycling plants. It could also offer a tax break or rebate to companies that process their used devices, and help prevent the export of e-waste to developing countries. However, a federal law is not likely to happen under this administration. A problem requiring multiple solutions With the flood of e-waste growing around the world, recycling alone will not be enough. Here are some other ideas and solutions that are being researched, considered or practiced around the world. "}}
