{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_255461441#2_492462717","relevance":0,"reason":"The passage is a municipal solid-waste and recycling page focused on local ordinances, transfer-station options, and maintaining a high general recycling rate. E-waste appears only as a drop-off heading and the result does not discuss challenges related to recycling e-waste.","learned":"Queries about recycling rates can drift into unrelated municipal recycling pages unless e-waste challenge language is tightly constrained."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"e-waste\" recycling (+ \"rare earth\" \"precious metals\" valuable) (+ challenges recovery separation))","reason":"Search for grade-3 evidence on material-recovery challenges, especially recovery/separation of valuable or rare-earth/precious materials from complex e-waste streams."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"e-waste\" recycling (+ \"rare earth\" \"precious metals\" valuable) (+ challenges recovery separation))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_361607669#3_840144869","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1644","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_361607669#3_840144869\" , \"end_char\": 3255.000000 , \"headings\": \" Old Electronic Devices Provide Precious Metals for Recyclers The Importance of Electronics Recycling<cover>Recovery of Precious Metals Recycling of E-Waste</cover>\" , \"segment\": \"Recovery of Precious Metals The recovery of these precious metals is no small issue. While a modern recycling facility can recover as much as 95% of gold, in developing countries, the crude dismantling processes employed may recover only 50% of this precious metal. Also, if done incorrectly, the recovery process can expose workers to a wide range of hazardous substances. Overall, the current recovery rates of e-waste for processing are quite small. For example, for 2009 the U.S. EPA reported that only 8% of cell phones were recycled, along with 17% of televisions, and 38% of computers. Not enough of overall devices find their way to recyclers, and for the ones that do, not enough metals are recovered from those devices, on a global scale. Recycling results in only a 10 to 15% recovery of all the gold stored "}}
