{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_458328791#5_496699650","relevance":3,"reason":"The document directly discusses e-waste dismantling and recycling challenges, including exports to countries with cheaper recycling due to lax environmental and workplace regulations, lack of federal/intergovernmental policies, hazardous materials in electronics, and public health risks from unregulated informal recycling in developing countries.","learned":"Key challenges include weak policy and regulation, export to countries with lax controls, informal-sector dismantling, hazardous materials such as heavy metals/flame retardants/plastics, and lack of proper equipment for safe breakdown of devices."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1648","reason":"This targeted informal-sector query produced strong direct evidence on export, regulation, hazardous materials, and unsafe dismantling, so continue to find more grade-3 evidence in the same vein."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1648"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_458289946#6_496645752","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1648","rank":2,"snippet":"devices to e-waste dealers. The informal recycling method consists mainly of manual, unskilled labor and is inherently mobile. Informal recycling operations also commonly occur in suburban areas where they lack effective enforcement and control. A main concern around the informal sector is that most peddlers and dealers lack knowledge and access to adequate equipment and technologies for safe e-waste disposal. Studies measured higher potential health risks from heavy metals at informal e-waste recycling sites than in formal recycling sites, such as those operating in Jiangsu and Shanghai. Nonetheless, it is a very profitable market in China thanks to low wages, high demand for used electronics, used parts and materials. While there have been centralized efforts to mitigate these risks through formalizing the e-waste management sector, the <cover>informal sector still dominates the e-waste collection system. Environmental and health impacts Most e-waste recycling </cover>sites in China lack the appropriate facilities to safeguard environmental and human health.\" , \"start_char\": 4714.000000 , \"title\": \"Electronic waste in China - Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electronic_waste_in_China\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"40/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_458289946#6_496645752","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is dedicated to electronic waste in China and directly supports key recycling challenges: informal recycling relies on manual, unskilled labor, lacks enforcement/control, knowledge, equipment, and safe technologies, has higher heavy-metal health risks than formal sites, dominates collection, and many recycling sites lack facilities to protect environmental and human health.","learned":"Informal e-waste recycling can dominate collection systems and create major challenges because operators often lack enforcement, knowledge, equipment, safe technologies, and facilities to control heavy-metal exposure and environmental harm."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1648","reason":"The informal-sector query is producing strong, direct evidence on major e-waste recycling challenges, so continue to the next result while it remains productive."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1648"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_458289946#7_496647737","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1648","rank":3,"snippet":"the informal sector is that most peddlers and dealers lack knowledge and access to adequate equipment and technologies for safe e-waste disposal. Studies measured higher potential health risks from heavy metals at informal e-waste recycling sites than in formal recycling sites, such as those operating in Jiangsu and Shanghai. Nonetheless, it is a very profitable market in China thanks to low wages, high demand for used electronics, used parts and materials. While there have been centralized efforts to mitigate these risks through formalizing the e-waste management sector, the <cover>informal sector still dominates the e-waste collection system. Environmental and health impacts Most e-waste recycling </cover>sites in China lack the appropriate facilities to safeguard environmental and human health. This results in the leaking of massive amounts of toxic chemicals, such as mercury, lead, and cadmium. Without the proper methods and necessary safety precautions, e-waste is directly responsible for deteriorating health and environment in China's e-waste hotspots. Residents in major e-waste recycling sites face a potential higher daily intake of heavy metal. Residents are exposed to the hazardous e-waste remnants though inhalation from air, dietary intake, soil/dust ingestion and skin contact. This "}}
