{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_458328791#6_496702648","relevance":3,"reason":"The document directly discusses e-waste export/recycling challenges, including unregulated informal recycling in developing countries, exported threats to human and environmental health, electronics composed of hundreds of materials, toxic heavy metals, and health hazards when devices are broken down without proper equipment.","learned":"Repeated evidence highlights unregulated informal recycling, hazardous materials in complex electronic devices, export of risks to developing countries, and lack of proper equipment for safe dismantling as key e-waste recycling challenges."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1648","reason":"The informal-sector query continues to produce directly relevant evidence on central e-waste recycling challenges, so continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1648"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_417129682#8_1002525031","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1648","rank":5,"snippet":"China) Tags: \" , \"segment\": \"A study done by the watchdog group Basel Action Network using trackers, however, found that 40 percent of the e-waste supposedly recycled in the U.S. was actually exported. Most of it ended up in developing countries—usually in Asia—where informal recycling is typically unlicensed and unregulated. At these <cover>informal recycling workshops, men, women and children recover valuable materials by burning devices to melt away non-valuable materials, using mercury and acids to recover gold, and dismantling devices by hand to reclaim other materials of value. Collecting wires which will later be burned in Guiyu, China. Photo: baselactionnetwork Usually they do not wear protective equipment and lack any awareness that they are handling dangerous materials. Research has found that inhaling toxic chemicals and direct contact with hazardous e-waste </cover>materials (even in some formal e-waste recycling settings) result in increases in spontaneous abortions, stillbirths, premature births, reduced birth weights, mutations, congenital malformations, abnormal thyroid function, increased lead levels in blood, decreased lung function, and neurobehavioral disturbances. Moreover, e-waste toxins contaminate the air, soil and groundwater. In the face of these health and environmental hazards, however, many people "}}
